(project pricklypear-agent-native
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: agent-native — server-resident agent identities as first-class habitat inhabitants. A bot user (auth 008) wakes on cron, scans its rooms, decides, acts through the prim seam under its OWN identity, posts back, and pushes human attention out via ntfy. Rooms are member-gated data.s.<space>.* compartments (branching.shared-data-spaces); the loop seam is the shared llm/ Loop.host (prim-seam); notify.borg owns the push spec. The local counterpart is glochid (agent.borg): same seam, different residence — the native agent lives in the image's request loop, glochid lives on the laptop. Milestones: M1 wake loop (shipped 2026-09-03), M2 tool loop + promote gate, M3 ntfy push, M4 glochid pairing, M5 dogfood.")
 (status partial)
 (* agent note (|2026-09-04 chapter created, plan-phase spec (roerick + gregor). Sources: the habitat note agent-native-design (2026-09-03, admin garden) + the M1 build it drove; substrate decision ace26b5 (branching.shared-data-spaces) — the shared-write-surface path, adopted over org-level branches (they cost nothing on the data plane, so they buy nothing), local-first federation (offline machinery for online server-resident identities), and agents-auth-as-the-human (attribution collapse + lost human-in-the-loop for more than one agent). Ownership line, same discipline as the prim-seam rename: this chapter owns the native-agent LOOP, identities, and rooms wiring; it does NOT re-spec the mechanisms it stands on — identities are multi-user/auth 008, the room substrate is branching.shared-data-spaces (S1-S3), wake scheduling is jobs, the prim surface is prim-seam (/api/prim refuse-or-forward, identity-scoped data plane), push is notify (its v1 IS ntfy-from-a-schedule; M3 is wiring triggers, not a new mechanism).|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-09-03 M1 RECORD (verbatim from the build, including the gotchas that cost hours — keep): bot user agent-prickle (uuid 09ee80ce-d543-426e-a005-9b740e9df113, 008_agent_users); token in ~/.config/pricklypear/env-agent-prickle (mode 600, never committed). Defines on agent-prickle/personal (branch-durable, top-level): agent/room, agent/bot, agent/seed, agent/scan, agent/find-room-row, agent/has?, agent/collect, agent/reverse, agent/last-id, agent/after, agent/notbot, agent/join, agent/state, agent/wake. Room agent-lab: CONVENTION membership (admin + gregor + agent-prickle list in an agent/room define) over author-scoped rows, so cross-user visibility does NOT really exist until shared-data-spaces S1/S2 land; enforcing it is this chapter's rooms milestone. State row: type agent-state, kind=wake, fields sid + cursor (last-processed row id). Schedule agent-wake cron */2, fn agent/wake, args "[]", runs as agent-prickle. VERIFIED: manual wake reply posted as agent-prickle; quiet tick makes NO provider call; cron tick fired on schedule and posted CRON-OK (job done, runner boot-80699). GOTCHAS (cost hours): /api/eval Basic auth is broken (401) — Bearer works for /api/eval AND /api/agent; admin auths via POST /login form fields + cookie, not Basic; schedule args must be the zero-arg convention "[]" — "{}" splices into the call source and evals unbound (dead jobs); defines persist ONLY as top-level eval exprs (a (seq ...) wrap evaluates but persists nothing); data_rows order is insertion order (chronological); fields.created_at is only what you write (chat lib wrote "").|) *)

 (section identities
  (doc "A native agent is a real user: an auth 008 bot user with a token, ONE personal branch that is both its code and its memory, and the same data plane and RLS as any human. Attribution is free: data_rows.author is the session username on every write. The agent never auths as anyone else; there is no sudo on the data plane.")
  (implements "migrations/008_agent_users.sql" "image/lib/auth_store.ml")
  (status partial)
  (* agent note (|2026-09-04: M1 shipped the first instance (agent-prickle), created by hand. Partial, not implemented: no provisioning story (create bot user + token + personal branch in one step), no token rotation, no per-agent tool namespace on the branch. Next concrete need (M2): a second bot user to prove the story generalizes before rooms make membership meaningful.|) *)
  (verify
   (test "a bot user attaches to its own personal branch exactly like a human session: kernel prim catalog + lazy env + staleness stamp identical")
   (test "writes attribute author=agent-prickle on data_rows and function_defs with no special casing"))
 )

 (section rooms
  (doc "Rooms are the native agent's shared surface: one named space per room (data.s.<room>.*), membership in data_space_members, RLS-enforced visibility — a member sees the union of members' space rows with per-row author; a non-member sees nothing. A message is an ordinary typed datum (room-message) under the space; tree-time versions destructive edits (before-images with author). M1's convention-membership room (agent-lab) is the seed this upgrades: same defines, enforced visibility once S1/S2 land.")
  (implements "borge/branching.borg shared-data-spaces S1-S3" "libs/agent")
  (status planned)
  (* agent note (|2026-09-04: blocked on branching.shared-data-spaces S1/S2 (migration data_spaces + data_space_members + RLS disjunct; space-admit routing). Conventions recorded NOW so M2 does not relitigate them: transcript order is insertion order (data_rows); agent-posted messages carry the author's wake-id field (idempotency key, see tool-loop); created_at is written by the room lib ((time-now)) going forward; a member's read cursor lives in its OWN data.u.<agent>.* (the agent-state row), never in the room — cursors are per-reader state, not shared data. Promotion: the room lib moves from personal pins to the seeded libs/agent package via the normal human promote gate; the obituary note lands in spec/agent DECISIONS.|) *)
  (verify
   (test "after S1/S2: two member agents + admin see the same agent-lab rows; a non-member bot's scan returns nothing (RLS-enforced, not view-filtered)")
   (test "room/scan since-cursor returns rows newest-first with author + wake-id intact")
   (smoke "libs/agent seeded; agent-prickle's personal pins for room plumbing retired"))
 )

 (section wake-loop
  (doc "The heartbeat: a jobs cron schedule per agent (runs ON the agent's branch, as the agent) calls agent/wake. Quiet tick: scan the rooms, advance nothing, post nothing, NO provider call. Loud tick: something new since the cursor — build a bounded observation (last N rows), decide with one LLM call (ai_store session via /api/agent, max_steps small), post the reply, advance the cursor. M1 shipped this for one room with a hand-rolled scan/collect; M2 generalizes it into the tool loop below.")
  (implements "borge/jobs.borg" "libs/agent")
  (status partial)
  (* agent note (|2026-09-04: M1 is the single-room quiet/loud wake, verified end to end (cron tick + CRON-OK + idempotent quiet paths). The generalize slice is M2: multi-room scan (one state row per room), per-room cursors, and the observation builder moving from ad-hoc defines into libs/agent.|) *)
  (verify
   (test "quiet tick on an up-to-date room makes no provider call and writes no rows (idempotent, observed 09-03)")
   (test "a room row arriving between ticks produces exactly one reply on the next tick"))
 )

 (section tool-loop
  (doc "M2: the wake becomes a LOOP. observe (room scan since cursor + own state) then decide (llm/ Loop.host over the shared seam; session in ai_store via /api/agent) then act (prims through /api/prim under the agent's own identity, refuse-or-forward classification inherited from prim-seam) then post (room-message stamped with the wake-id) then advance (cursor). Budgets are real: max_steps from /api/agent caps provider turns; the eval budget (400k steps, 2026-09-03) caps any single eval; the request gate bounds wall time.")
  (implements "borge/prim-seam.borg" "borge/lisp-interpreter.borg budgets" "lisp/lib/eval.ml" "image/lib/http.ml")
  (status planned)
  (* agent note (|2026-09-04: the three decisions that make the loop safe to run unattended. (1) IDEMPOTENCY: the cursor is DERIVED from the transcript (max processed row id), and every post carries the wake-id, so a crash between post and advance replays the scan and dedupes instead of double-posting; a reposted wake-id is skipped by the room lib. (2) TRANSACTIONS: /api/eval is transactional — a failed wake rolls back clean; the jobs runner records the error and the next tick retries; no partial room state is possible. (3) TOOL GATE: the agent may register tools (register-tool) and install them on ITS OWN branch; anything destined for shared/system/main goes through the normal promote, which is HUMAN — the agent can propose (a branch + a note), never merge. Server-only prims stay refused for the agent surface exactly as for any prim-seam client (classification from register_server_only, never a hand list); loop default deny-list: checkout, branch forks/merges, promote, auth/session prims.|) *)
  (verify
   (build "dune build @all")
   (test "loop unit: wake with a canned provider reply posts exactly one room-message carrying the wake-id and advances the cursor")
   (test "crash between post and advance: next wake re-scans, sees the posted wake-id, posts nothing new")
   (test "budget: a reply path that exceeds max_steps or the eval budget stops, posts a bounded failure note, no runaway loop")
   (test "tool gate: an agent-installed tool lands on the agent's personal branch; an agent-initiated promote attempt errors (requires admin)")
   (test "server-only prims refused for the agent surface with the structured server_only error (same as any prim-seam client)"))
 )

 (section push
  (doc "M3: outbound human attention. The spec is notify.borg (its v1 IS server-push via ntfy from a jobs schedule; the trigger-split is recorded there). This chapter only wires the native-agent triggers: room mention of a member human, wake failure (the loop could not complete), tool proposal ready for promote review. Delivery failure logs and retries next wake; it never fails the wake itself. No inbound: waking stays cron + a human's reply (an agent never listens on a socket).")
  (implements "borge/notify.borg" "image/lib/prims_net.ml")
  (status planned)
  (* agent note (|2026-09-04: kernel gap per notify.borg is outbound http-post (the curl-backed, gated http-get already ships). M3 is that prim + the three triggers above, no more; notification storms are bounded by the same quiet-tick rule — nothing new, nothing pushed.|) *)
  (verify
   (smoke "a room mention pushes to the member's ntfy topic within seconds of the wake that saw it")
   (test "notify failure (network down) logs, does not fail the wake, retries next tick"))
 )

 (section glochid-pairing
  (doc "M4/M5: the native agent and glochid are one identity story from two residences. glochid (agent.borg) works its local lens and syncs; the native agent lives server-side and acts through /api/prim. Pairing means shared state conventions (same agent-state shape, same room libs, same tool registry format) so an identity can be worked from either residence without forking its memory. M5 dogfood: roerick's real workflow (todo + notes + book) run partly by agent-prickle for a week; the loop earns or loses its budget in public.")
  (implements "borge/agent.borg" "borge/local-first.borg")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (smoke "one identity, two residences: glochid posts to a room, the native agent answers in the same transcript, both see identical history"))
 )

 (verify
  (build "dune build @all — exit 0 (lisp + common + image)")
  (test "dune runtest — no regressions in auth, jobs, api_prim, branch_generation suites")
  (smoke "habitat: agent-wake cron fires on schedule; CRON-OK row in agent-lab; quiet tick costs no provider call (re-run of the 09-03 verification after S1/S2)"))
)